 ## Overview
-Sum of Absolute Differences (SAD) is a regularization prior used in inverse volume rendering and tomographic reconstruction that extends Anisotropic Total Variation (ATV) by considering a larger local neighborhood. While ATV sums absolute differences along only the three principal axes, SAD sums the absolute differences between a voxel and all voxels in its immediate 3x3 spatial neighborhood (excluding the voxel itself), thereby enforcing smoothness across more edges per voxel [S1, S2, unofficial]. It is primarily chosen when stronger local smoothing is required than ATV provides, particularly in stochastic tomography reconstruction [S2, unofficial].
+Sum of Absolute Differences (SAD) is a regularization prior used in tomographic reconstruction that extends Anisotropic Total Variation (ATV) by considering a larger local neighborhood [S1, S2, unofficial]. While ATV sums absolute differences along only the three principal axes, SAD sums the absolute differences between a voxel and all voxels in its immediate 3x3 spatial neighborhood (excluding the voxel itself), thereby enforcing smoothness across more edges per voxel [S1, S2, unofficial]. It has been shown to produce excellent results in stochastic tomography reconstruction [S2, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
 **Inputs and Outputs**
