 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Computing the energy is $O(I \cdot J \cdot K)$, as each voxel processes a constant number (8) of neighbors.
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(1)$ auxiliary space if computed on-the-fly, or $O(I \cdot J \cdot K)$ if storing the gradient/neighbor differences.
-*   **Strengths:** Produces excellent results in stochastic tomography reconstruction compared to other priors [S2, unofficial]. It enforces stronger local smoothness than ATV by considering more edges [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** More computationally expensive than ATV due to the larger neighborhood. The proximal operator is more complex to implement than ATV's simple shrinkage [S1, unofficial].
-*   **When to use:** When ATV is too weak in smoothing and artifacts persist, particularly in tomographic reconstruction tasks [S2, unofficial].
-*   **When not to use:** When computational resources are extremely limited and ATV is sufficient, or when preserving sharp edges in all directions is critical (ATV might be preferred for anisotropic smoothing).
